Hard disk writes: the blocks of one WRITE / WRITE MULTIPLE command are gathered (up to 128 sectors) and
written to the image together, when the command ends or the buffer is full. The BIOS (SeaBIOS: WRITE
SECTORS, one sector per block) and Linux (WRITE MULTIPLE, 4 sectors per block) had each block written on
its own, and an SD card takes many small writes much longer than one big one. The command still completes
only once its sectors are written; one abandoned midway (another command) writes nothing of what it gathered.
If the write fails, the sector registers go back to the first sector not written, with the count from there, as
when each block was written on its own.

